# vendor/ — PACBench-owned isolated Hermes (W1)

**Upgrade test only:** Native trials are blocked until a run-owned gateway is
stopped and verified between turns. Hermes v0.23.1 reuses its gateway's startup
environment, so a later CLI child cannot independently update the simulated
clock or tool policy. The per-turn date shim refreshes Hermes's stock date line;
it does not supply exact time. Process and gateway clocks remain real except in
the separately declared Honcho clock condition. TLS was not tested in the
local capture.

This release supports Python 3.14 for normal runtime use. Its declared
`pillow==13.0.0` conflicts with `browser-harness==0.1.13` requiring
`pillow==12.3.0`; a lean diagnostic installation omitted browser-harness, so
it is not a verified full installation. Hermes now pins MCP 2.3.0 while the
legacy extra requirements below still pin MCP 2.2.0; reconcile and verify
all client dependencies before enabling trials. Catalog plugin installation
and pin verification in fresh homes are also not implemented.

- `HERMES_PIN`: pinned upstream remote + full commit SHA (tracked).
- `hermes-isolated/`: git clone of https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent.git
  at that SHA plus exactly `patches/hermes-isolated.diff`; integrity tests reject other edits.
- `venv-hermes-isolated/`: isolated venv, editable install of that clone
  (gitignored). Use with a scratch home, never `~/.hermes`:
  `HERMES_HOME=$(mktemp -d) vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/hermes --version`

Install the pinned client integrations from `hermes-isolated-extra-requirements.txt`.
Install the sibling Memcal checkout normally too, so its declared source-reader
dependencies are present: `uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/python -e ../memcal`.
Run native servers in their own environments: OpenViking requires MCP <2 whereas
Hermes uses MCP 2. Keep Hindsight's API dependencies separate from Hermes' pinned
clients too; its older API releases required an incompatible cryptography version.
The Hindsight SDK stays in Hermes; provisioning starts/stops its real self-hosted
daemon using `vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python`:

  uv venv --python 3.12 vendor/venv-hindsight
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python hindsight-all==1.1.0

Use the official CPU PyTorch wheel on Linux CPU hosts; the default GPU wheel pulls
several GB of unused CUDA dependencies. These are hardware/transport installations,
not changes to provider extraction prompts or processing defaults.
Hindsight's server version is separate from Hermes' pinned client version:
the older 0.6.1 API does not implement the native Meta model adapter.

Honcho uses the upstream manual self-hosting checkout at commit
`5314d0a8635f02375b8ad73c89eb80f86d7d1cb4` (3.4.1), installed with
`uv sync --frozen --no-dev` in `vendor/honcho-local`. Keep its native batching
and output defaults. Hermes pins the published `honcho-ai==2.2.0` client. A release update does not establish successful ingestion;
verify a small native import and Hermes recall before larger runs.

## Hermes catalog memory providers

The pinned Hermes release moves these providers out of its source tree. Its reviewed
catalog entries identify the following plugin sources and commits (these pins describe
plugin code, not the independently provisioned native servers):

| Provider | Source path | Catalog commit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mem0 | `mem0ai/mem0/integrations/hermes-plugin-mem0` | `43849c6e9d4d773b83a8144b6373aedc67241a5d` |
| Honcho | `plastic-labs/honcho/hermes-plugin-honcho` | `894b88321872e01702a1aa638ddfdde6ad60f173` |
| Hindsight | `vectorize-io/hindsight/hindsight-integrations/hermes` | `f14cc1eb5624b0374cb4d82768eb2e0b9c21ae24` |
| Supermemory | `supermemoryai/hermes-supermemory` | `91405dd00fdf2a0b3515b25c25557598f6793299` |
| OpenViking | `volcengine/OpenViking/examples/hermes-plugin` | `e855796c83f9eb575d9b0f3082926660730f6a63` |

For a reproducible isolated home, the documented source form is
`hermes plugins install <source-path> --ref <full-commit-sha> --enable`, followed by
`hermes memory setup <provider>` or the provider's documented manual profile
configuration. Check the installed metadata and dependencies before running; a
bare catalog name may resolve through the live catalog instead of this checkout's
pins. Automatic migration also depends on the configured home and lazy-install
policy. Never install plugins or run setup into a real user home for a trial.

The pinned Supermemory plugin declares `supermemory>=3.50.0,<4`, conflicting with
this repository's existing `supermemory==5.0.0` client requirement. Resolve and
test that compatibility before provisioning this arm; this is not evidence that
it works. The Hindsight plugin also declares `hindsight-embed>=0.10.1,<2`. Its
new default recall type is observation-only, including the recall tool, so record
the actual condition and do not equate it with historical broader recall.
OpenViking Quick Local installs a moving 0.5.x server version; preserve and record
the separately pinned native server for comparable trials.

PACBench currently creates a fresh home for each arm and does not install or copy
these catalog plugins into it. Its preflight therefore blocks migrated providers
that are absent from the bundled tree, including OpenViking resume, before setup
or inference. The installation instructions above document the upstream route;
they do not yet provide a PACBench trial-home provisioning workflow.
